    content:
      "Arbitration, in the context of high-stakes global commerce, is fundamentally chosen for its promise of expedited justice, yet disputes often arise where immediate, pre-tribunal relief is necessary to prevent irreparable harm. The figure who grants this relief is the Emergency Arbitrator (EA), a creature of institutional rules who can be appointed and pass an order in a matter of days.1 While the concept of the EA has been readily adopted by leading arbitral institutions worldwide, its legal status in India was, for many years, a source of profound doctrinal uncertainty, residing in a twilight zone where it was neither statutorily recognised nor routinely enforced.\n\nLegislative Silence and the Duplicative Justice Trap:\nIndia’s principal arbitration statute, the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 (A&C Act), remained deliberately silent on the Emergency Arbitrator, a mechanism that was proposed but ultimately not incorporated into the 2015 amendments. This omission created a gap: an EA order, though passed pursuant to institutional rules freely chosen by the parties, did not have a clear, direct path to enforcement under the Act. Parties who won an EA order were forced into a complicated legal workaround, typically having to file an application before an Indian court under Section 9 of the A&C Act to obtain an identical, parallel relief, thereby frustrating the very essence of quick, party-autonomy-driven arbitration.2 This uncertainty was a significant deterrent to parties considering India as an arbitration seat.\n\nThis judicial ambivalence was put to the test by the Supreme Court in 2021.3 The Supreme Court provided the much-needed clarity by unequivocally holding that an order passed by an Emergency Arbitrator in an India-seated arbitration is enforceable under the A&C Act. They achieved this by interpreting the EA order as an interim measure granted by an arbitral tribunal under Section 17(1) of the Act, which, by virtue of Section 17(2), is deemed to be an order of the Court and enforceable as such.4\n\nThe reasoning was the unwavering principle of party autonomy. If the parties had expressly agreed to the rules of the arbitral institution which contained the provision for an Emergency Arbitrator, they were bound by the outcome of that process. There is no express or implied bar in the A&C Act against the enforcement of such orders, thereby viewing the EA as an important step in aid of decongesting the civil courts and affording expeditious interim relief.\n\n1 Abhinav Gupta & Sriroopa Neogi, 'Emergency Arbitration in India: A Critical Appraisal of the Institutional Framework' (2021) 14 NUJS L Rev 641.\n2 Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996, s 9.\n3 Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd & Ors [2021] 10 SCC 416.\n4 Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996, s 17.\n\nFurthermore, potential delays were curtailed by ruling that an order for enforcement of the EA order under Section 17(2) is not appealable under Section 375 of the Act, which ensures finality and speed in the enforcement process.\n\nThe Incomplete Bridge and the Path to Parity\nThis definitive resolution consciously confined the jurisdiction to India-seated arbitrations, utilizing an expansive interpretation of the enforcement mechanism under Part I, specifically Section 17(2) of the A&C Act. Crucially, this judicial breakthrough did not extend its purview to Part II of the Act, which governs foreign-seated arbitrations. This fundamental distinction arises because EA measures, being inherently provisional and not final awards on the merits, fall outside the strict definition of an 'award' enforceable under the New York Convention.6 Therefore, the same principle of party autonomy that guaranteed swift relief domestically encounters a structural barrier internationally, and imposing a clear strategic risk on parties seeking urgent protective relief against assets located in India.\n\nSuch foreign EA orders are not directly enforceable as 'foreign awards' or 'interim measures' under the current statutory scheme. Consequently, a party must still seek relief under Section 9, using the EA order merely as highly persuasive evidence in an independent court application. This dichotomy shows us where legislative reform is still needed to align India's practice with true global standards and provide a comprehensive, clear mechanism for all emergency arbitral orders, irrespective of the seat, thereby ensuring that the legal bridge built is solidified by clear statutory mandate.\n\nThis significantly bolstered India's pro-arbitration credentials. This proved the Indian judiciary’s dedication to upholding party autonomy, transforming the enforceability of EA orders into a swift, non-appealable process for domestic arbitrations. This success, however, is geographically constrained to Part I. The Section 9 workaround sacrifices efficiency for critical protective measures. India’s strategic goal must be to legislate beyond this judicial fix. Only through the explicit statutory recognition proposed in the Draft Bill, extending direct enforcement to all seats, can India genuinely secure its position as a globally consistent arbitration hub. and this also ",
